        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Heat Pump Dryer vs Vented vs Condenser: What Actually Differs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A vented dryer heats air with an element, tumbles your clothes through it, and blows the humid result outdoors. The same things that make it fast, a hot airstream and a constant supply of replacement air, also make it wasteful, because that replacement air is conditioned air pulled out of your living space and sent outside.
          </p>

          <p>
            A heat pump dryer closes the loop. A compressor moves refrigerant between an evaporator and a condenser. Warm air picks up moisture from the clothes, passes over the cold evaporator where the water condenses out, and is then reheated by the condenser and sent back through the drum. The water drains to a tank or hose, and the same air circulates for the whole cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            A conventional condenser dryer also works without a vent, but it heats the air with an electric element and cools it with room air through a heat exchanger. It is ventless and simple, yet it spends roughly as much energy as a vented dryer and dumps heat into the room. The heat pump version recycles that heat instead, which is the entire reason to pay more.
          </p>

          <p>
            In plain terms the three categories are a trade: vented is fastest and cheapest, condenser is flexible but inefficient, and heat pump is the efficient, gentle, ventless choice that dries more slowly. A heat pump tumble dryer is the same thing under its UK name; American listings simply call it a ventless heat pump dryer.
          </p>
        </div>

